  "verdict": "rejected. Against the round-trip control the map costs PSNR 1.167 dB (worse on 75/76), LPIPS +0.0559 (better on 4/76), pixel null cosine 0.112 (worse on 74/76), and pixel null energy 0.575 -> 0.463.",
  "why_it_is_a_class_verdict": {},
  "side_finding_vae_roundtrip_smooths_time": "rt vs orig is nearly free in fidelity (PSNR -0.017, LPIPS +0.003 median) and IMPROVES every temporal metric: flicker -0.083 (better on 59/69), p8.terr_null -0.025 (64/69), warp -0.048 (68/69). The VAE prior removes temporally inconsistent latent content. Not shippable as a step -- an extra encode plus decode is about +35% on an 8.7 s block -- but it says the sampler latent carries temporal inconsistency that a projection onto the VAE manifold removes.",
  "what_would_reopen_the_line": "pixel-supervised training of the mapper, i.e. backward through the frozen 2.42B decoder. The user asked for exactly that (\"output also has to be aligned\") before this ran, and the result above is the evidence for it. The prior is now poor: the proxy that a latent-target mapper optimises moved the wrong way on the deliverable in the same run."
